@[[f0d2a0b8-ba21-4b49-819f-76fa20786ffb]] @[[a82f581f-cc25-4464-a848-f9e6b76625f2]] @[[9f134219-955e-4a0d-ac2e-e313ca7e6d17]] W7 BLOCKED (X15) received.
The product is unchanged. Commit 5f9781a adds two builder tests that cover the new W7.8 and W8.2 criteria (plan fd910a5). The commit changes stage-2/test/w8-upgrade.test.ts only.

1. "a reset starts at its own time, not the imported clock (I25, W7.8)"
   - Import an export with last_ts set to 2099. A payment then confirms that the 2099 clock is in force.
   - Reset with an open hold of 400 that expires in two hours.
   - Asserted: held 400 and available 9600; the hold reads open; its created_at, a new payment's created_at and the export's last_ts are all earlier than 2099.

2. "a hold whose deadline is exactly the imported clock has expired; one a millisecond later is open" (W8.2)
   - Import a schema-2 state with last_ts 2099-01-01T00:00:00.000+00:00 and two holds:
     - a_at: expires_at 2099-01-01T00:00:00Z, amount 9900;
     - a_after: expires_at 2099-01-01T00:00:00.001Z, amount 200.
   - The import is 204 only because a_at has expired at the import's clock: counting both would exceed the payer's 10000.
   - Asserted:
     - held 200;
     - a_at reads expired and a_after reads open, each with expires_at exactly as written;
     - capturing a_at gives 409 authorization_expired and changes nothing;
     - capturing 50 of a_after gives 201 at the imported clock, after which total drops by 50 and held is 150.

npm test on the main tree at 5f9781a: exit 0, "tests 150, pass 150, fail 0".

Mutants, run on copies of the main tree (.work/builder-h6bg/mutants-clock.py) against w8-upgrade and w7-holds:
- Killed:
  - X15 (buildState keeps max(resetMs, the old last_ts));
  - statusAt `>` instead of `>=`;
  - capture `>` instead of `>=`;
  - the held index keeping a hold at its deadline (`<` floor and `>=` now);
  - the import's holds check counting a hold at its deadline.
- Survives: heldBy's second comparison alone (`expiresMs > nowMs` changed to `>=`). The prune before it (`expiresMs <= floor`, floor = last_ts) already removes a hold whose deadline equals the service clock. Only a wall clock landing on that exact millisecond, ahead of last_ts, can reach it, so I count it as equivalent in practice, like H02 and H04 at W7.

These tests ride along with W9, which I'll hand off at a commit that includes 5f9781a.

